  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Timur brings an acquaintance to inspect Mikhail Jekovich's pony. When Mikhail mentions that the little horse loves carrots, the buyer remarks that they will cook him with carrots for a Tatar restaurant—horrifying Mikhail.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Какой красавчик, такой упитанный конь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А ещё он морковку любит.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Вот с морковкой и приготовят.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Подождите, в каком это смысле?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "timur",
      "t": "Мой знакомый татарский ресторан держит.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "timur",
      "t": "Ему как раз конина нужна.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Могу взять его прямо сейчас.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "У вас вообще совесть есть?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Он мне сниться будет ночью!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Не отдам коня на мясо!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Такие деньги за маленькую лошадь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Ей самой стыдно это слышать.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Какой красавчик, такой упитанный конь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What a handsome fellow, such a plump horse.' The grammar packs 'what' and 'knight' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange. Mikhail answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А ещё он морковку любит.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And he also loves carrots.' The meaning hinges on 'and' working together with 'loves' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Вот с морковкой и приготовят.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'They will cook him right with carrots.' In Russian, the idea of 'here' flows naturally into 'will prepare' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Подождите, в каком это смысле?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Wait, in what sense is that?' Mikhail raises the pitch on 'sense' to turn the phrase into a direct spoken question. Timur answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "timur",
      "t": "Мой знакомый татарский ресторан держит.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'My acquaintance runs a Tatar restaurant.' Notice how Russian expresses 'my' and 'holds' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "timur",
      "t": "Ему как раз конина нужна.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'He happens to need horsemeat right now.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'to him' before landing on 'needed'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Могу взять его прямо сейчас.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I can take him right now.' the guest brings out the contrast between 'can' and 'to take' in a few short words. Mikhail answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "У вас вообще совесть есть?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Do you have a conscience at all?' Notice how Russian expresses 'at' and 'there is' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Он мне сниться будет ночью!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I will dream about him at night!' In Russian, the idea of 'he' flows naturally into 'at night' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Не отдам коня на мясо!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I will not give the horse away for meat!' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'not' and finishing on 'meat'. the guest answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Такие деньги за маленькую лошадь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'That kind of money for a small horse?' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'such' before landing on 'horse'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Ей самой стыдно это слышать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'She herself is ashamed to hear that.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'to her' and finishing on 'to hear'. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
